Field Deployment Protocol: Autonomous Artifact Ingestion & Reconstruction (v.3.1)
1. Mission Parameters & Node Topology
 * Target Coordinates: 45.783^\circ\text{N}, -122.654^\circ\text{W} (Localized off-grid wilderness testing quadrant).
 * Hardware Footprint: 10 ruggedized, solar-powered edge nodes (N_1 through N_{10}) deployed in a ring-mesh topology using LoRa and Wi-Fi HaLong sub-GHz peer-to-peer transceivers.
 * Primary Objective: Ingest, cryptographically verify, and collaboratively reconstruct a perishable organic artifact (e.g., an organic fiber composite or degraded wooden implement fragment) without relying on centralized cloud infrastructure.
2. Phase 1: Ingestion & Telemetry Capture
 * Sensor Array Activation: When a localized node discovers or receives a physical artifact sample, the onboard macro/microscopic optical sensor array and chemical spectrometer scan the sample.
 * Feature Extraction: The hardware extracts high-resolution volumetric geometry, surface wear patterns, and organic chemical signatures, converting them into a standardized digital telemetry packet TP_0.
 * Post-Quantum Signing: The collecting node signs TP_0 using its post-quantum cryptographic private key (e.g., ML-DSA/Dilithium), establishing immutable data origin verification right at the edge.
3. Phase 2: Distributed State Reconstruction & Verification
 * P2P Broadcast: The signed telemetry packet is broadcast across the 10-node mesh network.
 * Decentralized Verification: Each node runs the state reconstruction validation module, evaluating the incoming data against the continuous-time trust engine parameters:
   $$\max(0.0, \min(1.0, \int_{0}^{t} e^{-\lambda (t - \tau)} \left[ \alpha \cdot C_i(\tau) + \beta \cdot V_i(\tau) - \gamma \cdot P_i(\tau) \right] d\tau))`
 * Consensus Check: Nodes cross-reference the submitter's eigenvector centrality trust score (T_i). If T_i exceeds the operational threshold and zero-knowledge identity proofs validate, the state commitment is appended to the local distributed ledger.
4. Phase 3: Recursive Analysis & Autonomous Simulation
 * Multi-Physics Simulation Kernel: The mesh collective pools its compute resources (via decentralized containerized orchestration) to simulate the artifact's degradation timeline, stress tolerances, and material composition.
 * Anomaly & Integrity Screening: The anomaly detector checks for data corruption or tampering attempts. If any node attempts to inject false structural data, its anomaly rate (\Delta a_i) spikes, immediately triggering the exponential penalty equation P_i(\tau) = \exp(k \cdot \Delta a_i) - 1 to isolate the corrupted node from the consensus pool.
5. Phase 4: Execution & Consensus Finalization
 * Liquid Governance Consensus: The 10 nodes execute a lightweight quadratic voting protocol to ratify the reconstructed 3D digital model and historical attribution of the artifact.
 * Permanent State Commitment: Once consensus is achieved across \ge 80\% of active nodes, the finalized artifact blueprint is mirrored across the offline mesh storage array, completing the closed-loop autonomous cycle entirely in the wild.
